	affiliations = {Babeș-Bolyai University, Cluj-Napoca, Romania},
	abstract = {This article explores the family transmission of conservatism in the context of perceived intergenerational social mobility in a sample of parents and their young adult children in Hungary. Using a modified 40-item Wilson-Patterson conservatism scale, it analyzes the association of perceived intergenerational mobility, both experienced and expected, to parent–child congruence in issue positions, conservatism and three dimensions of conservatism (economic socialism, cultural liberalism and social conservatism). Results show that young adults who expect to be downwardly mobile are more liberal than their parents, especially on the economic socialism dimension. On the other hand, those who expect to be upwardly mobile are more conservative on both the economic and cultural liberalism dimensions. In the case of the social conservatism dimension, measuring support for traditional sexual morality and Church authority, neither the experience nor the expectation of social mobility significantly decreases young adults’ congruence with their parents. These results suggest that certain facets of ideology are more resistant to the pressure of perceived intergenerational social mobility. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
