	abstract = {Recent theoretical progress has widely recognized the dynamic, time-changing nature of place attachment, with late childhood to adolescence identified as a critical period for its development. Moreover, the psychological processes emerging from person-place interactions were theoretically considered as the foundation of the development of place attachment. Despite these theoretical advancements, there is still a notable lack of empirical research examining heterogeneity in developmental trajectories of place attachment, as well as the psychological predictors associated with this heterogeneity, which has hindered our understanding of place attachment from a developmental perspective. This exploratory study employed a three-wave longitudinal quantitative investigation among Chinese children (N = 1267; M<sub>age at Time 1</sub> = 12.46, SD<sub>age</sub> = 2.46; 49.09% girls). Growth mixture modeling revealed three distinct developmental trajectory classes of place attachment, namely the High and Decreasing Class, Moderate and Stable Class, and Low and Increasing Class. Additionally, machine learning approaches were employed to explore the psychological predictors of place attachment trajectories among 43 candidate predictors across individual, home, school, and societal contexts. The top 10 psychological predictors associated with heterogeneity in place attachment trajectories were identified, including life satisfaction, perceived social mobility, length of school, childhood unpredictability, and peer friendship. These findings provided empirical insights into developmental theories of place attachment and laid the groundwork for future research on its dynamic nature and antecedents. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
