	affiliations = {Department of Sociology and Human Geography, University of Oslo, Oslo, Norway},
	abstract = {This study presents a theoretical framework for an empirical analysis of the links between intergenerational mobility and emigration willingness and intentions. Existing research shows that immigrants are likely to experience social mobility in host societies. Still, virtually no studies indicate how upward and downward intergenerational mobility, taking place before individuals leave their countries, are associated with emigration. Referring to economic, sociological, and psychological theories and using nationally representative and comparative survey data for 35 countries in Europe, the Middle East, and Central Asia, we fit diagonal reference models to test whether intergenerational mobility in educational attainment and subjective perception of intergenerational mobility have significant associations with general emigration willingness and specific emigration intentions. The results suggest that subjective perception of intergenerational downward mobility has statistically significant links with emigration willingness and intentions, but this association is attenuated in more economically developed countries. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
