	affiliations = {College of Education, Fuyang Normal University, 678 Qinghe West Road, Fuyang, China},
	abstract = {Grounded in Ecological Systems Theory, this study examines the link between subjective socioeconomic status (SES) and depressive symptoms and the underlying mechanisms, that is, the mediating role of upward social mobility belief and the moderating role of Zhongyong thinking (Doctrine of the Mean) among 552 Chinese university students. Results showed that higher subjective SES was associated with fewer depressive symptoms, and this relationship was mediated by stronger upward social mobility belief. Crucially, Zhongyong thinking moderated the subjective SES – upward social mobility belief link: the association was significantly stronger among students with higher Zhongyong thinking; and the upward social mobility belief – depressive symptoms link: the association was significantly stronger among students with higher Zhongyong thinking, whereas it did not significantly moderate the relationship between subjective SES and depressive symptoms. These findings highlight the interplay of perceived social standing and culturally rooted cognitive styles in establishing the association with mental health and social aspirations, underscoring the importance of integrating Confucian values like Zhongyong thinking into culturally responsive psychological models and interventions. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2026.},
