	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042639493?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {“Regional Economics and Management” Department, National University of Uzbekistan named after Mirzo Ulugbek, Uzbekistan; Bioeconomy Research Institute, Vytautas Magnus university, K. Donelaičio g. 58, Kaunas, 44248, Lithuania},
	abstract = {The adoption of artificial intelligence in transition economies raises questions about whether technology diffusion promotes inclusive development or amplifies existing inequalities. We examine associations between AI use, human capital accumulation, and perceived social mobility using survey data from 1,142 respondents in Uzbekistan. Weighted regression models with two-way clustered standard errors indicate that AI adoption is positively associated with self-reported digital competencies and participation in professional development, although these associations vary substantially across population subgroups. The connection between these factors is largely mediated by access to technology and training, though for the most disadvantaged groups, this link effectively vanishes and becomes statistically insignificant. Structural equation modeling indicates that perceived mobility relates to AI use primarily through human capital pathways, with human capital emerging as a central mediating mechanism. Access to institutional support, particularly active labor market programs, strengthens associations between human capital and perceptions of mobility. Our analysis reveals that technology diffusion provides only a partial account of the outcomes. Without support from investment in education and infrastructure, achieving inclusive growth will remain difficult. The findings provide empirical evidence on skill-biased technological change in developing-economy contexts while highlighting the methodological challenges inherent in establishing causality from observational data. © 2026, Czestochowa University of Technology. All rights reserved.},
