	abstract = {As brands diversify their product lines, it is vital to ensure consumer acceptance of distant brand extensions. This research introduces perceived social mobility as a novel antecedent influencing response to distant brand extensions. Seven studies (including one field study and six preregistered experiments) demonstrate that high (vs. low) perceived social mobility enhances cognitive flexibility, facilitating greater acceptance of distant brand extensions. Study 1, a field study on TikTok, ensures the ecological validity of these findings. Studies 2a, 2b, and 2c confirm cognitive flexibility as the underlying mechanism via measurement-of-mediation and process-by-moderation approaches. Study 3 establishes that the effect applies to distant extensions but not near extensions. Study 4 distinguishes distant brand extensions from related constructs such as paradox brands. Study 5 identifies a key boundary condition: when cognitive load is high, the effect of perceived social mobility attenuates. These insights augment research on perceived social mobility, brand extension, and consumer cognition, while offering practical marketing strategies for introducing distant brand extensions. © Academy of Marketing Science 2026.},
