	abstract = {Objective: This study examined the relationship between perceived economic inequality (PEI) and dispositional envy, along with its underlying mechanisms and boundary conditions. Methods: We recruited 286 Chinese adults (Study 1) and 448 Chinese adolescents (Study 2) to examine the relationship between PEI and envy, the mediating effects of a zero-sum mindset and perceived social mobility, and the moderating effect of socioeconomic status (SES). Results: In both adults and adolescents, PEI was positively associated with malicious envy across SES backgrounds, while its relationship with benign envy varied by SES. A zero-sum mindset mediated the linkages between PEI and both forms of envy in adults, whereas, in adolescents, it solely mediated the relationship between PEI and malicious envy. Unexpectedly, subjective social mobility was positively associated with PEI across age groups and exerted a suppressing effect on the relationship between PEI and both forms of envy in adolescents, fostering benign envy and suppressing malicious envy. Conclusion: These findings highlight the diverse strategies for economic inequality across SES groups and explicate the role of the zero-sum mindset in exacerbating malicious envy and subjective social mobility in promoting benign envy. © 2025},
